{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בקמת רעך", :translit "b'kamat re'ekha", :gloss "the standing grain of your fellow"} {:he "כי יקח איש אשה", :translit "ki yikach ish ishah", :gloss "when a man takes a wife"} {:he "מה שדה נקנה בכסף ושטר אף קדושין", :translit "mah sadah nikneh b'chesef ushtar af kiddushin", :gloss "just as a field is acquired by money and deed, so too kiddushin"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhat parshiyot: the juxtaposition of adjacent verses yields a legal inference"}], :frame {:text "The method of juxtaposition (semikhah) draws legal conclusions from the thematic link between adjacent passages. The laws of kiddushin are derived by analogy to land acquisition, which the Mishnah states can be effected by money, a deed, or hazakah. The Talmud derives that a woman is acquired through kiddushin by these same methods, drawing the comparison from the term 'field' (sadeh) used in the context of marriage in Exodus 22:16.", :sources ["Mishnah Kiddushin 1:1" "Kiddushin 2a-b" "Exodus 22:16"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhat parshiyot: the juxtaposition of adjacent verses yields a legal inference", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:38:46.194184799Z", :model "glm-5.2"}